Hi Jessica,
My first reaction is that what you are trying to do does not really add value to the graph and the message you are trying to convey, so you probably want to think about whether or not you really want to go down that road. As for being able to execute the gradient fill as you suggest in ggplot2, I am not sure how to do it. Gradients and the like usually map to data, not something to make the chart look more "fancy." I just did a Google search to see if there is anything out there on this sort of thing and came across this question on SO and it looks like others have already responded and have a similar take on this. FYI, it is generally considered bad style to post here and on SO without suggesting as such and providing a link to the SO question. Not everyone tracks both sites so you don't want people spending time constructing responses if you already have your solution.

Best wishes,
Doug

